<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  launchd template for the external committee-health trigger.

  WHY LOCAL AND NOT A CLOUD CRON: GitHub's `schedule:` trigger fires far too sparsely to be coverage.
  Measured in this repo: the first schedule event arrived 8.1 h after the workflow landed
  (registration delay), then 2 fires across ~31 due slots in the 7.7 h after that, about 7%. That
  cannot catch the failure this watches — the real incident on 2026-08-31 lasted 12.8 minutes.

  CORRECTED, twice, and both retractions are kept here as the reason rather than deleted:

  1. This block used to say the cron "was measured not to fire (83min/5 slots/0; 6h33m/26 slots/0)".
     Wrong. Both of those windows sat entirely inside the registration delay above, so neither could
     have observed a firing. A window in which the first event has not happened yet is not a
     delivery rate of zero.

  2. It also used to say a local timer's failure mode is DECIDABLE — "if this Mac is asleep or off,
     nobody is watching and you know it". Also wrong, and disproved on the same day: the Mac did not
     power off, it CLAMSHELL SLEPT. launchd SKIPS StartInterval firings while asleep (it does not
     catch up) and caffeinate does not cover Clamshell Sleep, so closing the lid stopped this monitor
     (2 slots skipped, 15:53:12Z → 16:38:28Z, matching pmset sleep 16:03:21Z → DarkWake 16:34:46Z) at
     the same moment it stopped the keeper this monitor exists to watch. Nobody knew until someone
     touched the laptop. A monitor that shares a failure mode with its subject is not a monitor.

  So install this as the best available trigger, NOT as a solved problem: each run now reports the
  slots it missed, which makes the blind window visible afterwards but cannot close it. The actual
  fix is not hosting the keeper and its monitor on a machine that sleeps.

  INSTALL (not done for you — this puts a persistent agent on your machine):
    sed -e "s|REPO_PATH|$PWD|g" -e "s|HOME_PATH|$HOME|g" \
      ops/com.aastar.committee-health.plist.template \
      > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.aastar.committee-health.plist
    launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.aastar.committee-health.plist
  VERIFY — ask launchd, not the file. `plutil -lint` only says the XML is legal: a StartInterval
  nested one level too deep lints green and is never scheduled (dvt hit exactly this, and the
  "self-heals every 120s" their comment promised had never once happened). The ABSENCE of the
  run interval line IS the bug:
    launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.aastar.committee-health | grep -E "run interval|last exit"
    tail -f ~/Library/Logs/committee-health-poke.log
  REMOVE:
    launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.aastar.committee-health.plist
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.aastar.committee-health</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-lc</string>
    <!-- login shell so gh + its keychain auth resolve exactly as they do in a terminal -->
    <string>cd REPO_PATH &amp;&amp; ./scripts/committee-health-poke.sh</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>900</integer>
  <!-- Run once at load so a broken install is visible immediately, not 15 minutes later. -->
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><true/>
  <!-- launchd's own spawn errors only. The run history lives in ~/Library/Logs/committee-health-poke.log,
       written and rotated by the script itself, because /tmp is periodically cleaned on some macOS setups. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>HOME_PATH/Library/Logs/committee-health-launchd.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>HOME_PATH/Library/Logs/committee-health-launchd.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
